{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":21059,"ns":0,"title":"Constructivism (philosophy of mathematics)","extract":"In philosophy of mathematics, constructivism asserts that it is necessary to find (or \"construct\") a specific example of a mathematical object in order to prove that an example exists. Contrastingly, in classical mathematics, one can prove the existence of a mathematical object without \"finding\" that object explicitly, by assuming its non-existence and then deriving a contradiction from that assumption.","description":"Philosphical view that existence proofs must be constructive","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}